"""The page contract and output of `graphic`, as `graphic --help` prints it."""

HELP = """\
Render a local HTML page to a graphic FilmCraft composites over the edit: a transparent ProRes 4444 MOV (straight alpha, tagged BT.709, the sequence's exact frame rate, the page's sound as 48 kHz PCM), or one transparent PNG still.
Frame size and rate come from the sequence (--project, or --bridge for the running app; --sequence for one that is not active), or from --size and --fps when there is no sequence yet (a motion graphic on its own).

The page
- Viewport: the frame size in CSS pixels at device scale 1. Leave html and body without a background: what is not painted stays transparent.
- Time: frame i is captured at page time t = i / fps. Before each frame, window.seek(t) is called if the page defines it (and awaited if it returns a Promise); then every animation the page had when it finished loading (CSS @keyframes, Web Animations) is set to t, counting from the moment the page opened (delays included); then two animation frames pass and the frame is captured. Animations are frozen otherwise: nothing runs on the clock while rendering. Animations created later, for instance inside seek, are seek's to set.
- Frames are captured in any order and each must depend on t alone: not on the wall clock (Date.now, performance.now, requestAnimationFrame timestamps), unseeded randomness, or earlier frames. CSS transitions run on the wall clock and are refused (exit 1): use @keyframes or seek.
- Length (.mov): --duration S, or window.duration in seconds, set by the time the page has loaded. Frames = length x fps rounded to the nearest frame (half up), at least 1; the movie lasts frames / fps (3 s at 30000/1001 is 90 frames, 3.003 s).
- Sound (.mov): define window.renderAudio(sampleRate, seconds) returning an AudioBuffer, or a Promise of one (an OfflineAudioContext's startRendering()). It is called once, before any frame, with 48000 and the movie's length; its time 0 is the first frame. A longer buffer is cut and a shorter one padded with silence. It must not change what the page draws. Return null for no sound.
- Loading: file: and data: URLs only; paths relative to the page work. Network requests are blocked and listed in `blocked`, so a re-render months later draws the same frames: keep fonts and images next to the page. The first frame waits for the page's load event, its fonts and every <img>.
- Colour: the page is sRGB. In an HDR sequence (HLG, PQ) FilmCraft converts the render like any SDR clip, SDR white at the HDR reference level, so a page is written the same way for any sequence.

Replacing a render
--out is written beside itself and verified first; an earlier file at --out stays byte for byte until then, and on any failure. FilmCraft keeps the length and sound it read when a file was imported, so a clip placed from the earlier file plays a re-render of the same length, sound, size and rate as is; `replaced` and `next` say when it must be cleared and placed again.

Prints: {"out", "kind": "video"|"still", "size": "WxH", "fps" (.mov), "frames", "seconds" (.mov), "coverage": [{"t", "painted", "opaque"}] (first, middle and last frame; painted = share of the frame with any paint, opaque = with alpha >= 250, which is what hides the picture), "audio": {"peak_dbfs", "lufs"} or null (no sound; levels are null for silence), "fonts": [{"family", "drawn_by": [{"face", "glyphs"}], "fallback", "text" (when fallback)}] (every visible text, sampled at the start, each whole second and the end; fallback = the family is missing or lacks some glyphs and another face drew them), "blocked": [urls], "replaced": null (no earlier file) or {"length_changed", "audio_changed", "size_changed", "fps_changed"}, "took_s", "next": {"steps": [filmcraft-cli commands that import and place the render, with <placeholders>], "placeholders", "if_placed" (when replacing), "note"}}.
Placing: the steps put the render on a new video track and its sound on a new audio track, leaving A1's speech alone (timeline.place without audioTrack overwrites A1; graphics.newFromFile drops the sound).

Exits: 0 written; 2 the command line must change (a .mov without a length, a length that is not a positive finite number, --out not .mov or .png, --out the page itself, no page, --fps written as a decimal, --size without --fps); 1 the render failed and `error` starts with the stage: launch (Chrome missing), load, files (a local file the page asked for is missing), fonts, images (one cannot be decoded), page (an uncaught error or unhandled rejection), seek (seek or the settle after it failed or timed out, or a CSS transition started), audio, capture, encode (ffmpeg), verify (the written movie differs from what was asked for); or filmcraft-cli failed reading the sequence.
"""
